                        "glosses": [
                            "Alternative form of Rowleian.",
                            "Relating to the literary persona of Thomas Rowley."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "cb913d31-5874-43a4-8645-9cca6831bf07",
                                "sense_id": "8cf45933-2a3b-3176-a91b-f59f5be27921",
                                "text": "The work's increased use of Rowleyan language makes the act of reading unavoidably self-conscious as we can no longer rely on our usual ways of making sense of poetry."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "4f661ac6-87c0-44f5-9f14-bc2fe5d37e02",
                                "sense_id": "8cf45933-2a3b-3176-a91b-f59f5be27921",
                                "text": "It seems clear that Chatterton worke out a theory of the history of English drama that would cilminate in Rowley's Ælla. We have inferred a similar theory of prosodic history from the uncertainty and irregularity of pre-Rowleyan prosody as compared to Rowley's correctness."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "c25d52cb-848f-4686-97da-206c38f8769f",
                                "sense_id": "8cf45933-2a3b-3176-a91b-f59f5be27921",
                                "text": "So Chatterton, by breaking his apprenticeship, deftly steered a course between the Bristolian and the Rowleyan voices: between 'Matrimony he shall not contracct' and 'an hollie Preeste unnmarriageabil'."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
